---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import TimeSeriesChart from "../components/TimeSeriesChart.astro";
import pwi from "../../public/data/pwi_latest.json";

function fmt(n: number | null | undefined, d = 3) {
  if (n === null || n === undefined || Number.isNaN(n)) return "n/a";
  return n.toFixed(d);
}
function signed(n: number | null | undefined, d = 2) {
  if (n === null || n === undefined || Number.isNaN(n)) return "n/a";
  return (n >= 0 ? "+" : "") + n.toFixed(d);
}
---
<Base title="Probability Weighting Index (PWI)" description="Trade-count-weighted non-bot Prelec alpha from 640M Polymarket trades.">
  <h1>Probability Weighting Index (PWI)</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Trade-count-weighted non-bot Prelec alpha. Alpha = 1 is rational pricing; below 1 is the classical inverse-S weighting.</p>

  <p>
    As of <strong>{pwi.as_of}</strong>, the PWI stands at <strong>{fmt(pwi.value, 3)}</strong>,
    with a 52-week rolling z-score of <strong>{signed(pwi.z52w, 2)}</strong>.
    13-week moving average: <strong>{fmt(pwi.ma13w, 3)}</strong>.
    52-week moving average: <strong>{fmt(pwi.ma52w, 3)}</strong>.
    Based on <strong>{pwi.n_trades_nonbot_week.toLocaleString()}</strong> non-bot trades this week.
  </p>

  <h2>Weekly history</h2>
  <TimeSeriesChart
    dataUrl="/data/pwi_timeseries.json"
    yField="pwi_alpha"
    smoothField="pwi_alpha_ma13w"
    yLabel="non-bot Prelec alpha"
    zeroLine={false}
  />
  <p class="muted" style="font-size:14px; margin-top:-18px;">
    Light line: weekly alpha. Bold line: 13-week moving average. An alpha of 1.0 would mean the market prices binary outcomes rationally.
  </p>

  <h2>Components (weighted averages across non-bot wallets)</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Component</th><th>Latest value</th><th>What it captures</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Trade-weighted mean calibration error</td><td class="mono">{fmt(pwi.components.weighted_mean_cal_error, 4)}</td><td>Absolute gap between trade price and realized frequency</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Trade-weighted longshot fraction</td><td class="mono">{fmt(pwi.components.weighted_longshot_fraction, 4)}</td><td>Share of trades at prices below 10%</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>52-week rolling statistics</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Statistic</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Mean</td><td class="mono">{fmt(pwi.rolling.mean_52w, 3)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>SD</td><td class="mono">{fmt(pwi.rolling.sd_52w, 3)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Min</td><td class="mono">{fmt(pwi.rolling.min_52w, 3)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Max</td><td class="mono">{fmt(pwi.rolling.max_52w, 3)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Observations</td><td class="mono">{pwi.rolling.n_obs_52w}</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Methodology</h2>
  <p>
    Each week, a one-parameter Prelec weighting function w(p) = exp(-(-log p)^alpha) is fit by weighted
    nonlinear least squares separately to each wallet class (active retail, sophisticated, casual, one-shot,
    bot). The PWI is the trade-count-weighted mean of the alphas across all <strong>non-bot</strong> wallet
    classes that week. Bots are excluded (<em>trades_per_day</em> &gt; 50 OR <em>n_trades</em> &gt; 1000) so
    the index reflects human probability weighting rather than algorithmic liquidity provision.
  </p>
  <p>
    Interpretation: an alpha of 1.0 corresponds to rational pricing with no probability distortion. An alpha
    below 1 is the classical inverse-S weighting (small probabilities over-weighted, mid probabilities
    under-weighted) documented in Tversky and Kahneman (1992). An alpha above 1 implies the rarer opposite
    pattern. The 52-week rolling z-score requires at least 13 non-null observations and is blank before that.
  </p>

  <h2>Data</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/data/pwi_history.csv">pwi_history.csv</a>
    {' '}({pwi.n_weeks_history} weeks, from {pwi.first_date})
    &middot;
    <a href="/data/pwi_latest.json">pwi_latest.json</a>
    &middot;
    <a href="/data/pwi_timeseries.json">pwi_timeseries.json</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Cite</h2>
  <div class="citation">{`@misc{dellavedova2026pwi,
  title        = {Probability Weighting Index},
  author       = {Della Vedova, Joshua},
  year         = {2026},
  version      = {0.1.0},
  publisher    = {University of San Diego},
  url          = {https://jdellavedova.com/pwi},
  note         = {Part of the Della Vedova Prediction Market Indices (DV-PMI)}
}`}</div>
</Base>
